# Summary
Introduced: the commit changed history retention from whole-epoch protection to per-route pruning, but did not update floor validation or runtime-map generation to distinguish complete history records from partial route-only records.
The new pruner keeps only exact protected `(epoch, PG)` routes in old, non-floor-protected history records. Once unrelated PG routes are removed, the code has no marker that the history record is partial. A later storage-node heartbeat can set a `cluster_map_history_floor_epoch` covering that same epoch. The validation path accepts the request because `cluster_map_at_epoch(required_epoch).is_some()` only proves the epoch exists, not that all PG routes are present. The refresh response then iterates only the remaining `history.pgs()` entries, silently omitting pruned routes that the floor request expected to be available. An authenticated or faulty storage node can therefore cause incomplete historical runtime maps, potentially breaking placement/backfill/recovery decisions and causing availability or data-integrity issues.

# Resolution
The finding was valid at commit `908108d2`, but was fixed by the subsequent exact-route retention and sparse-history work:

- [`d479b4c0`](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/d479b4c0fa12089173d7cb2f97a47878a02b9f1e) replaces the storage node's aggregate history-floor retention input with durable exact `(kind, epoch, PG)` route references.
- [`940da076`](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/940da07692279691b773efcf24e90b1cbdf39ef0) retains each exact referenced route and its metadata-transfer dependency closure and reconstructs exact routes for filtered storage-node refresh responses.
- [`cedad691`](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/cedad6916728a4996d749625df8f4cdf85c75593) makes sparse history unambiguous by encoding reverse PG-route deltas, explicit PG-introduction absence boundaries, and a canonical retained-epoch list in runtime-map RPC v6.

Heartbeat validation now reconstructs every exact referenced route and rejects a reference that is unavailable. Persisted-state validation applies the same per-route requirement. Runtime-map refresh starts from those exact references, follows transfer dependencies, and reconstructs the required routes rather than treating the oldest reference as a request for every PG route in every later epoch. A route therefore need not be physically repeated in an epoch record, but it must remain reconstructible from the reverse deltas and absence boundaries.

`cluster_map_history_floor_epoch()` remains as an aggregate diagnostic derived from the exact references. It is not used as the history-retention capability and does not permit a later request for complete history from that epoch.

Verified on the fixed implementation with:

- `cluster_map_history_pruning_preserves_only_exact_storage_node_route`
- `storage_node_refresh_filters_unrelated_history_around_exact_old_route`
- `heartbeat_persists_exact_cluster_map_history_route_references`
- `exact_old_route_retains_later_pg_introduction_boundary`

All four focused tests passed.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the changed pruner can mutate complete history records into partial per-route records without a completeness marker.
- [x] Demonstrate a retained history range can contain every required epoch while records are missing unrelated PG routes.
- [x] Demonstrate storage-node heartbeat floor validation accepts that partial range because it checks only epoch existence.
- [x] Demonstrate persisted/publication validation also accepts the partial range for the same reason.
- [x] Demonstrate runtime-map refresh/reconstruction exposes the impact: missing PG routes are not restored or reported as a floor-validation error, and refresh output silently omits them.
## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust unit-test PoC added to `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs` and archived as a patch. Relevant code evidence: `prune_cluster_map_history` mutates retained records by `record.pgs.retain(...)` with exact `(epoch, pg)` route keys and does not mark records partial (`crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:14965-14999`). Heartbeat floor validation only checks `snapshot.cluster_map_at_epoch(required_epoch).is_some()` (`crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:14256-14279`). Persisted/publication validation likewise builds a set of retained epochs and checks only epoch membership (`crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:13283-13326`). Runtime-map refresh iterates only `history.pgs()` and therefore cannot notice PGs pruned out of a partial record (`crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:1635-1655`).

The PoC test `poc_partial_route_history_satisfies_floor_and_omits_pg_routes` constructs 257 complete records with PG1 and PG2, invokes the real `prune_cluster_map_history` with exact-route protection for PG1 only for epochs 1..256, and proves the resulting 256 retained records are continuous but all missing PG2. It then sets a node floor to epoch 1. Both `validate_storage_cluster_map_history_floor(...).unwrap()` and `validate_required_cluster_map_history(...).unwrap()` succeed, even though `reconstructed_pg_route_at_epoch(pg2, floor_epoch)` returns `UnknownPg`. Finally, `runtime_map_for_storage_node_refresh(...)` succeeds and the returned historical routes contain PG1 but no PG2, demonstrating silent omission.

Command output: `cargo test -p storage poc_partial_route_history_satisfies_floor_and_omits_pg_routes -- --nocapture` => `test control_plane::tests::poc_partial_route_history_satisfies_floor_and_omits_pg_routes ... ok` and `1 passed; 0 failed`. Valgrind was attempted but unavailable (`bash: command not found: valgrind`). ASan was attempted via `RUSTFLAGS='-Z sanitizer=address'` but stable rustc rejected nightly `-Z`. gdb was unavailable, but lldb was run non-interactively with a regex breakpoint and produced a stack trace reaching the PoC test.

